{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "the social network (2010) directed by david fincher is a masterfully crafted film that dissects the birth of facebook and the cutthroat world of tech entrepreneurship with razor-sharp precision. the screenplay by aaron sorkin is a linguistic tour de force, packed with rapid-fire dialogue that crackles with wit, ambition, and venom. jesse eisenberg’s portrayal of mark zuckerberg is nothing short of brilliant—he captures the genius, social awkwardness, and moral ambiguity of the character with unsettling accuracy. andrew garfield and armie hammer deliver equally compelling performances as edwardo savarin and the winklevoss twins, respectively, adding layers of charm, rivalry, and bitterness to the narrative.\n\nthe film’s visual style is quintessential fincher: sleek, dark, and meticulously composed. the cinematography by jeff cronenweth bathes the story in a cold, blue-tinged palette that mirrors the sterile, cutthroat atmosphere of the tech world. the editing, handled by kirk baxter and angus wall, is brisk and rhythmic, perfectly syncing with the dialogue’s pace. trent reznor and atticus ross’s haunting, pulsating score amplifies the tension, making the film feel like a high-stakes chess match played out in neon-lit offices and ivy-covered campuses.\n\nthe story unfolds like a modern-day shakespearean tragedy, where ambition, betrayal, and intellectual property battles dominate the plot. the legal and personal fallout of zuckerberg’s actions serve as the backdrop for a meditation on friendship, loyalty, and the cost of success. the film doesn’t shy away from portraying zuckerberg as a flawed, even unsympathetic figure, yet it never reduces him to a mere villain. instead, it presents him as a complex product of his environment—a brilliant but socially stunted young man who sees the world through code and algorithms, often at the expense of human connection.\n\none of the film’s greatest strengths is its ability to make the intricacies of software development and corporate law engaging. the courtroom scenes and deposition sequences are as tense as any thriller, filled with verbal sparring that feels like a battle of wits. the supporting cast, including brenda song as zuckerberg’s long-suffering girlfriend, christy lee, and max minghella as his business partner, add depth and authenticity to the world-building.\n\nif there’s any criticism to be leveled, it’s that the film occasionally feels like it’s rushing through certain characters or subplots to keep the pacing tight. for instance, the relationship between zuckerberg and his friend eduardo savarin is given short shrift in the second half, which feels like a missed opportunity to explore the emotional toll of their falling out. nevertheless, these are minor quibbles in an otherwise flawless film.\n\nthe social network is more than just a biopic or a tech drama—it’s a scathing, darkly humorous, and deeply human story about the birth of a digital empire and the personal sacrifices made along the way. it’s a film that feels as relevant today as it did upon its release, serving as both a historical account and a cautionary tale about the dark side of innovation. with its razor-sharp dialogue, stellar performances, and flawless direction, it’s a modern classic that deserves to be studied, dissected, and rewatched for years to come.\n\nin summary: a near-perfect blend of drama, comedy, and tragedy, the social network is a must-watch that transcends its subject matter to deliver a universally resonant story about ambition, betrayal, and the price of greatness.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.3156192898750305}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.002393240574747324}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " (", "logprob": -0.9320786595344543}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.00047434045700356364}, {"token": "0", "logprob